  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, if $f(x),g(x)\\in G,$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f+g)(0) & =f(0)+g(0) \\\\  & \\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $G$ is closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, if $f(x)=1$ for all $x\\in [0,1]$ and $k=-1\\in R,$ then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(kf)(0) & =((-1)f)(0) \\\\  & =-f(0) \\\\  & =-1 \\\\  & <0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $G$ is *not* closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is false, while statement IV is true. $G$ is *not* a subspace because it's not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and IV only.\"",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
